1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===History 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://monctonpermanentsettlers1766.ca/people/charles-jones/
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Jones/Schantz
2 CONT About the Jones Family Name: Johann Carl Schantz, Born in Gondelsheim, Germany in Oct (?) 1721. Anglicized in Philadelphia: Charles Jones
2 CONT
2 CONT Origin: Gondelsheim, Germany (note: for a time, he was thought to be Welsh since Jones is the most common name in Wales, but modern research has tracked down his origins to Johannes Schantz aka John Jones: born August 23, 1753 in 
2 CONC Pennsylvania — died 1810
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Jones: Born about 1758 – died January 20, 1840
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Margretha Schantz aka Margaret Jones: Born December 1769 in Philadelphia – died
2 CONT
2 CONT Catharina Elisabeth Schantz aka Catherine Elizabeth Jones: born July 1765 in Philadelphia – died April 1, 1854
2 CONT
2 CONT Other Information: Originally given a large distribution of oats to establish a gristmill. Died 1774 in Moncton. Somewhere between 1775 – 1780 Jacob Reicker Sr may have married Charles`s widow.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Carl and Jacob Schantz appear on a 1749 passenger list for the Lydia, Capt. John Randolph, from Rotterdam to Philadelphia in October 1749. Carl signed the passenger list with a “+”, the same way signed the Articles of Agreement to
2 CONC  go to Monckton in 1766.[3][4] He married Anna Margaretha Lindtz in Philadelphia's St. Michael's Lutheran Church on December 6, 1752.[5] The marriage was witnessed by her brother Michael Lintz and her brother-in-law, Christoph Web
2 CONC er.[6] In 1765 he was a resident of Roxborough, Pennsylvania along with fellow settlers Matthias Sommer, Michael Lutz and Heinrich Stief.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Schantz family were among the original group of German families in Philadephia contracted by John Hughes of Benjamin Franklin & Co. to settle Monckton Township on the Petitcodiac River. Their ship, the single-masted sloop “Lov
2 CONC ey” under Captain Nathaniel Shiverick, landed at Hall's Creek in present-day Moncton, New Brunswick on June 3, 1766.[8][9] Jones received Lot Four of the new township, situated on Mill Creek (now Somers Creek) giving him an energy
2 CONC  source for his gristmill.[10] Charles died in Moncton about 1774, and Lot Four was given to his two sons Henry and John.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Jones was long believed to have been of Welsh origin, and his presence as the only non-German of the early Monckton Township settlers was a mystery. The discovery of Pennsylvania baptismal records of his children using his
2 CONC  original German name Schantz, witnessed by members of the Stieff and Lutz families who later settled Moncton with him, led to research into his German origins.[12]
