1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Per Jönsson Silkelock==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Birth:	1550 inKristdala, Oskarshamn, Kalmar (H), Sverige (Sweden)
2 CONT * Citizen, councilor and ship captain in Kalmar .. Was about 76 years.
2 CONT * Death:	before December 02, 1626 in Systertorp, Bankhult, Kristdala (H), Sverige (Sweden)
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Married==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married: '''Elisabeth Jönsdotter Sparre över Pil,''' daughter of Jöns Gustafsson Sparre över pil and Lucia Persdotter 
2 CONT * Cohabitation with '''Ingeborg Knutsdotter Sabel (1589 - 1664)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Children==
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Per Silkelock,''' (1580? -)
2 CONT * '''Elisabet Persdotter,''' (1600? - 1662)
2 CONT * '''Gustav Persson Silkelock,'''. (- <1655)
2 CONT
2 CONT ==History==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Citizens in Kalmar, located there 1584-1609 (SmH 1584: 6 f 13, Swedish National Registry 1609 3/11 vol 108 f 392v, RA)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Per in 1586 together with a Byriel Bengtsson gets the right to build stone houses in the city (National Register 1586 4/7 vol 71 f 245v, RA). Sampling in the local customs accounts shows that he is a frequent exporter of, for exam
2 CONC ple, tar, butter, tallow, ox and cowhides, goat and goatskin, horses and wood of various kinds; imports consisted of different types of fabric, salt, lead and powder (see, for example, Local Customs Accounts, Kalmar 1590, vol. 492
2 CONC : I f 19, 34v, RA). In the Civil War he is said to have been on King Sigismund's side (see the evidence produced by Olle Elm and Bo Persson from Hertigh Carl's Butcher's Bench above) but seems to have been taken at the mercy of Du
2 CONC ke Karl after the end of the war when he was called 'our ship captain' (1605). 1609 3/11 vol 108 f 392v, RA). He is missing in 1609 years of team mating, uncertain why (SmH 1609: 3B).
2 CONT
2 CONT Per Silkelock's wife is never known so well known in the contemporary sources, but she was certainly the daughter of the deputy chief Jöns Gustavsson at Strömsnäs, Högsby sn. This one buys Systertorp around 1590 and gets fixed on 
2 CONC the farm -92 (Bankeström 1982 p. 296, 1997 p. 269). During the 1610s, two persons with the surname Silkelock are mentioned as living in Systertorp. Both are riders. Gustav Silkelock in Systertorp is stated in 1618 to be the daught
2 CONC er of Jöns Gustavsson and his wife Lucia (Bankeström 1997 p 269). The rider Per Silkelock is certainly located in Systertorp 1615 (Älvsborgs solution 1613: 3, RA). Per Silkelock's surname and connection to Systertorp means that he
2 CONC  too should be a descendant of Jöns Gustavsson. The most reasonable interpretation then is that he is Gustav Silkelock's brother. The connection to Kristdala is strengthened by the fact that a daughter - Elisabet, is pledged as ma
2 CONC rried to Bartholdus Nicolai Duraeus in Kristdala (Linköping's archive, vol. E IV: 163 [Målilla 1631-1750], dated 1652 26/11, see Bo Persson's transcript above, for the case's continuation see Bankeström 1997, p. 268). A note in Ka
2 CONC lmar's customs accounts in 1590 according to which Jöns Gustavsson bought free beer "to his daughter's wedding" is interesting in this context (Local customs accounts, Kalmar 1590, vol 492: I f 72v, RA). Through the note on Gustav
2 CONC  Silkelock in 1618 we know that Jöns Gustavsson had at least one daughter. If it is the same daughter in 1590 then we would have the marriage year for Per Silkelock. However, since Jöns Gustavsson may have had several daughters, w
2 CONC e cannot be sure. see Bo Persson's transcript above, for the continuation of the case see Bankeström 1997 p 268). A note in Kalmar's customs accounts in 1590 according to which Jöns Gustavsson bought free beer "to his daughter's w
2 CONC edding" is interesting in this context (Local customs accounts, Kalmar 1590, vol 492: I f 72v, RA). Through the note on Gustav Silkelock in 1618 we know that Jöns Gustavsson had at least one daughter. If it is the same daughter in
2 CONC  1590 then we would have the marriage year for Per Silkelock. However, since Jöns Gustavsson may have had several daughters, we cannot be sure. see Bo Persson's transcript above, for the continuation of the case see Bankeström 199
2 CONC 7 p 268). A note in Kalmar's customs accounts in 1590 according to which Jöns Gustavsson bought free beer "to his daughter's wedding" is interesting in this context (Local customs accounts, Kalmar 1590, vol 492: I f 72v, RA). Thro
2 CONC ugh the note on Gustav Silkelock in 1618 we know that Jöns Gustavsson had at least one daughter. If it is the same daughter in 1590 then we would have the marriage year for Per Silkelock. However, since Jöns Gustavsson may have ha
2 CONC d several daughters, we cannot be sure. Through the note on Gustav Silkelock in 1618 we know that Jöns Gustavsson had at least one daughter. If it is the same daughter in 1590 then we would have the marriage year for Per Silkelock
2 CONC . However, since Jöns Gustavsson may have had several daughters, we cannot be sure. Through the note on Gustav Silkelock in 1618 we know that Jöns Gustavsson had at least one daughter. If it is the same daughter in 1590 then we wo
2 CONC uld have the marriage year for Per Silkelock. However, since Jöns Gustavsson may have had several daughters, we cannot be sure.
2 CONT Source: Roger Axelsson 
2 CONT
2 CONT Written for Fallebo nr 1, Kristdala sn, 1607-1615. (Veine Sjöö, History of five villages in Kristdala sn, 1993, p. 120.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Sources==
2 CONT
2 CONT Bosatt i Kalmar, belagd där från 1584 till åtminstone 1592. Skeppskapten 1609. Senare eventuellt bosatt i Systertorp och Bankhult, Kristdala sn. Källa: "Humlenäsboken" (2001), s 156.
2 CONT
2 CONT Per Silkelock  - http://www.laneborg.se/minantavla/pf9521091.html
