1 OCCU Lärare, Författare
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Konstnär, lärare, författare.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elsa Andrea Elisabeth Björkman-Goldschmidt (1888–1982) was a Swedish artist and writer who was active in Sweden and Austria. After attending Stockholm's Art Academy, she worked as an engraver and etcher. In 1916, while assisting t
2 CONC he Red Cross in Russia, she met her future husband, the Austrian surgeon Waldemar Goldschmidt. They married in Vienna where she was involved with Save the Children and started working as a correspondent for the Swedish press. In 1
2 CONC 938, anti-Semitism forced the couple to move to Sweden where she published a number of books about her life in Vienna.[1][2][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT Born in Linköping on 16 April 1888, Elsa Andrea Elisabeth Björkman-Goldschmidt was the daughter of the army officer Daniel Magnus Fredrik Björkman and his wife Maria née Heyman.[1] After following a teacher training course at the 
2 CONC Anna Sandström Seminary in Stockholm (1906–08), she travelled abroad to improve her language skills. She then spent a year at the Art Academy concentrating on etching, woodcut and lithography (1909–10). She spend a further year st
2 CONC udying graphic arts in Belgium, making study trips to Italy and Germany.[2] She presented her work at exhibitions in Sweden and abroad.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1916, she travelled to Russia with her humanitarian friend Elsa Brändström to work an as untrained nurse in the Siberian prisoner-of-war camps. She returned to Russia a number of times in subsequent years as a delegate of the S
2 CONC wedish Red Cross, experiencing the Russian Revolution. She met her husband to be, the Austrian surgeon Waldemar Goldschmidt, in a Moscow hospital. After marrying in Vienna in 1921, they settled in the city where they became involv
2 CONC ed in various cultural associations. Elsa Björkman-Goldschmidt turned increasingly from art to writing, contributing columns to the Swedish daily Dagens Nyheter. In 1938, after the Nazis annexed Austria, as Jews they were forced t
2 CONC o return to Sweden.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Björkman-Goldschmidt wrote accounts of her years in Vienna in a series of books published from the 1940s. Titles included Det var i Wien (1944), Donaurapsodi {1945), Wien vaknar (1949) and Följ med till Wien (1959).[1] Excepts hav
2 CONC e been translated into German by the historian Renate Schreiber and published in 1982 as Es geschah in Wien. Erinnerungen von Elsa Björkman-Goldschmidt.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Elsa Björkman-Goldschmidt died in Stockholm on 6 April 1982.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:  Wikipedia
